    "article": "**When Quota Attainment Lies to You: Reading the Numbers More Carefully**\n\nQuota attainment is one of the most common metrics in sales compensation. On the surface, it looks simple: divide a rep's actual revenue by their assigned quota, and you get a percentage. If a rep closes $900,000 against a $1,000,000 quota, their attainment is 90%. Simple, right?\n\nNot quite. In practice, quota attainment data can tell very different stories depending on how you read it.\n\nConsider a sales team where 75% of reps hit their quota in Q3. Leadership might celebrate this result. After all, three out of four reps performed well. But a data scientist looking more carefully might ask: what was the distribution of attainment? If most reps landed between 95% and 105%, the team is performing consistently. But if half the reps are at 130% and the other half are below 60%, the average hides a serious problem. The quota-setting process may be broken.\n\nThis is called **attainment distribution analysis**, and it is one of the most useful tools in revenue operations. Instead of only tracking whether reps hit quota, you study the full curve of outcomes. A healthy distribution typically looks like a bell curve centered near 100%. A skewed or bimodal distribution often signals that quotas were set without enough data, or that territory assignments are unequal.\n\nAnother important concept is **pay-for-performance alignment**. Compensation plans are designed with the assumption that hitting quota means earning the target commission. But if too many reps consistently exceed quota by large margins, the company is likely underpaying top performers. Conversely, if reps consistently fall short, the plan may be demotivating rather than driving behavior.\n\nA real-world example: a software company analyzed two years of attainment data and found that enterprise reps in the Northeast region consistently hit 140% of quota, while reps in the Midwest averaged 68%. The quotas had been set using national averages without adjusting for market size or existing account penetration. The fix was not to reduce enterprise rep pay. It was to recalibrate quotas using regional data.\n\nThis kind of analysis requires combining compensation data with pipeline data, territory maps, and historical close rates. It is exactly the intersection where data science and revenue operations meet.\n\nThe key takeaway is this: quota attainment is a signal, not a verdict. High attainment can mean strong performance — or it can mean quotas were set too low. Low attainment can mean poor execution — or unfair territory design. Your job as an analyst is to separate the signal from the noise.",
    "writing_task": "Write **2–3 sentences** summarizing the main argument of the article.\n\nYour sentences should answer these two questions:\n- What is the problem with reading quota attainment as a simple number?\n- What should analysts do instead?\n\nUse your own words. Do not copy sentences directly from the article. Try to include at least one of these phrases: *\"the article argues that\"* or *\"according to the author.\"*",
